;; Endeavor 149 rung 1 — the SMALLEST kernel whose backward needs a staged tile's PRIMAL.
;;
;; TDD: this spec is expected to FAIL until primal replay exists.  Today it fails with the
;; engine's (correct) refusal:
;;
;;     cannot differentiate: the backward needs the PRIMAL value of A-TILE, a scratch tile
;;     that is not filled by load-tile-at, so its contents cannot be recovered (a backward
;;     kernel replays the forward's bindings but not its statements).
;;
;; That refusal is the STARTING LINE, not a bug.  Until replay exists it is strictly better
;; than the alternative: a backward that reads the empty tile and silently returns zero.
;;
;; WHY A PRODUCT AND NOT A COPY.  A copy would not exercise this at all — d(copy)/dx = 1 needs
;; no primal value, and the generic set!/~ chain already carries it (139/02 proves that).  The
;; backward only needs a tile's CONTENTS when the tile is an operand of a MULTIPLY:
;;
;;     C[i] = A-tile[i] * B-tile[i]
;;     dA-tile[i] = B-tile[i] * dC[i]     <- needs B-tile's numbers
;;
;; and B-tile is empty in the backward.  That is the whole endeavour in four lines.
;;
;; DELIBERATELY NO MMA, NO SWIZZLE, NO RINGS.  146 learned the cost of debugging an AD gap
;; through a kernel with three other features in it (the 142 ring work cost five iterations).
;; Rung 2 adds the non-invertible index map; rung 3 adds the safety constraint.
;;
;; THE CHECK.  f(A,B) = sum(A*B) elementwise, so dA[i] = B[i].  With B[1] = 1.5, dA[1] = 1.5.
;; Chosen so a wrong answer is unambiguous: an empty-tile read gives exactly 0.0, and a
;; confusion between A and B gives 2.0 (A[1]).  Neither can be mistaken for the right answer.
;;
;; NOTE ON group=4.  VERIFY-AUTODIFF defaults to group=1 — ONE thread.  Without this key the
;; kernel still gives the right answer (the single thread strides over all four elements) but
;; it proves nothing about staging: one thread fills every slot and then reads every slot, so
;; no tile value ever crosses a thread boundary and the sync-workgroup is decorative.  A
;; replay mechanism that got the cross-thread case wrong would pass anyway.
;;
;; COMPILE-WITH[--ir-target=spv]: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--ir-target=spv --differentiate]: PASS
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS
;; VERIFY-AUTODIFF: A=[1.0 2.0 3.0 4.0] B=[0.5 1.5 2.5 3.5] at.A=1 expect.A=1.5 at.B=1 expect.B=2.0 h=0.5 atol=1e-3 output-vec=4 group=4

(def-type vec-t (vector float :address-space :global :align :compact))

(def-kernel staged_product (A B &out C)
  (declare #'(vec-t vec-t &out vec-t)
           (global-size :set-to 4)
           (local-size  :set-to 4))
  (let ((A-tile (make-scratch-vector float 4))
        (B-tile (make-scratch-vector float 4)))
    ;; HAND-STAGED: a plain set!, not load-tile-at, so nothing records where these came from.
    (workgroup-stride A-tile (i)
      (set! (~ A-tile i) (~ A i))
      (set! (~ B-tile i) (~ B i)))
    (sync-workgroup)
    ;; The multiply is what makes the backward need the tiles' CONTENTS.
    (workgroup-stride C (i)
      (set! (~ C i) (* (~ A-tile i) (~ B-tile i))))))
